Good shift in topic — here's a minimal custom block with a heading, paragraph, and image, using the modern approach (JavaScript + block.json).

**1. block.json** (in a plugin folder, e.g. `wp-content/plugins/my-block/`):
```json
{
  "$schema": "https://schemas.wp.org/trunk/block.json",
  "apiVersion": 3,
  "name": "myplugin/hero-card",
  "title": "Heading + Text + Image",
  "category": "layout",
  "icon": "format-image",
  "editorScript": "file:./index.js",
  "style": "file:./style.css"
}
```

**2. index.js** (build with `wp-scripts build`):
```js
import { registerBlockType } from '@wordpress/blocks';
import { useBlockProps, MediaUpload, MediaUploadCheck } from '@wordpress/block-editor';
import { Button } from '@wordpress/components';
import { __ } from '@wordpress/i18n';

registerBlockType('myplugin/hero-card', {
  attributes: {
    heading: { type: 'string', default: 'Your heading' },
    text: { type: 'string', default: 'Your paragraph.' },
    imageUrl: { type: 'string', default: '' },
  },
  edit({ attributes, setAttributes }) {
    const blockProps = useBlockProps();
    return (
      <div { ...blockProps }>
        <h2><input value={ attributes.heading }
          onChange={ e => setAttributes({ heading: e.target.value }) } /></h2>
        <textarea value={ attributes.text }
          onChange={ e => setAttributes({ text: e.target.value }) } />
        <MediaUploadCheck>
          <MediaUpload
            onSelect={ media => setAttributes({ imageUrl: media.sizes?.medium?.url || media.url }) }
            allowedTypes={['image']}
            render={({ open }) => (
              <Button onClick={ open } variant="secondary">
                { attributes.imageUrl ? 'Change image' : 'Select image' }
              </Button>
            )}
          />
        </MediaUploadCheck>
        { attributes.imageUrl && <img src={ attributes.imageUrl } alt="" /> }
      </div>
    );
  },
  save({ attributes }) {
    const blockProps = useBlockProps.save();
    return (
      <div { ...blockProps }>
        <h2>{ attributes.heading }</h2>
        <p>{ attributes.text }</p>
        { attributes.imageUrl && <img src={ attributes.imageUrl } alt="" /> }
      </div>
    );
  },
});
```

**3.** In your main plugin PHP file: `add_action('init', fn() => register_block_type(__DIR__));`

**4. Build:** `npm init -y && npm install --save-dev @wordpress/scripts` then add `"scripts": { "build": "wp-scripts build" }` and run it.

Activate the plugin and the block appears in the inserter as "Heading + Text + Image." If you'd rather skip the build tooling, I can show you a quick `wp.blocks.registerBlockType` via `enqueue_block_editor_assets` instead — just say so.
